nd/building-a-coding-agent-64","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-the-spine-already-provides","content":"Many calls in one step"},"1132":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-65","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-the-spine-already-provides","content":"The model writes a program against the tool bindings"},"1133":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-66","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-the-spine-already-provides","content":"codeMode"},"1134":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-67","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-the-spine-already-provides","content":"Big tool catalogues, large results, budgets"},"1135":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-68","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-the-spine-already-provides","content":"Deferred loading, spill with head and tail, per-session ceilings"},"1136":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-69","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-the-spine-already-provides","content":"deferredTools, spillPolicy, budget"},"1137":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-70","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-the-spine-already-provides","content":"Language server"},"1138":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-71","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-the-spine-already-provides","content":"A seam and a tool; bring a provider"},"1139":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-72","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-the-spine-already-provides","content":"ctx.lsp, lspTool"},"1140":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-73","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-the-spine-already-provides","content":"Health endpoint"},"1141":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-74","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-the-spine-already-provides","content":"/health"},"1142":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-75","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-the-spine-already-provides","content":"@agentkit/server"},"1143":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-76","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-stays-in-your-product-layer","content":"These are product decisions, and a second product would want them different. Build them on the seams above rather than waiting for a config key."},"1144":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-77","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-stays-in-your-product-layer","content":"The shell. Terminal UI, tabs, a chat sidebar, diff review, a login flow. Render from session/event; @agentkit/react has the cards if you are in React."},"1145":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-78","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-stays-in-your-product-layer","content":"The daemon. A long-lived process that owns the runtime, mounts handleSessionRoutes behind your auth, and maps users to sessions through its scope callback."},"1146":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-79","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-stays-in-your-product-layer","content":"Titles and metadata. A title, a cwd, a model label per session. Keep them in your own store keyed by session id, or log them as your own event type and project them."},"1147":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-80","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-stays-in-your-product-layer","content":"A job queue with retries and priority. ctx.jobs runs work in the background; a durable queue across restarts is a product choice (pg-boss, BullMQ, or a small table)."},"1148":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-81","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-stays-in-your-product-layer","content":"Remote workers. AgentKit is in-process. Codex-style isolated environments are your container around a runtime, with the sandbox providers inside it."},"1149":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-82","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-stays-in-your-product-layer","content":"Horizontal scale. One runtime per user or machine is the model that fits. A shared event bus across processes is a different design."},"1150":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-83","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-is-still-missing","content":"Ordered by leverage. Each names the seam it would plug into."},"1151":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-84","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-is-still-missing","content":"Durable approvals. tools/ask waits in-flight. A run that needs approval while the user is away should end the turn with a logged tool/approval-required fact and resume when a later request carries the decision. Needs a resume path in the loop; it is the right next runtime change and the one TrueForge has that AgentKit chose not to port yet."},"1152":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-85","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-is-still-missing","content":"runId on events. Sub-runs and parallel delegates are correlated today by session id and time. A run id on turn/start and its children would make a task tree trivial to render."},"1153":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-86","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-is-still-missing","content":"apply_patch. A unified-diff tool beside write_file for models that prefer patches. Small, and it belongs next to gitTools since git apply is the natural implementation."},"1154":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-87","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-is-still-missing","content":"Codebase-aware retrieval. retrieval is generic. A preset that watches a root, respects ignore files and exposes search_codebase is a plugin over the existing vector stores."},"1155":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-88","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-is-still-missing","content":"A published CLI. mini-harness is the reference; promoting it to a package with the settings-files convention is packaging, not runtime work."},"1156":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-89","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#what-is-still-missing","content":"Wire parity for the spine. @agentkit/server serves createAgent sessions. handleSessionRoutes covers the log; a send, cancel and approve trio over the spine's AgentHandle would let @agentkit/client talk to a harness too."},"1157":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-90","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#a-minimal-v1","content":"Add your own tools with defineHarnessTool, your own prompt sections, and a tools/ask responder that talks to your UI. Everything above runs on the mock provider for tests, and dumpConfig-style printing with describeBundle shows anyone what you shipped."},"1158":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-91","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#related","content":"Context engineering"},"1159":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-92","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#related","content":"Extending AgentKit"},"1160":{"id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent-93","page_id":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/building-a-coding-agent#related","content":"Testing audit, including what remains weak"},"1161":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","type":"page","content":"Bundles","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Extending"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/bundles"},"1162":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-0","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles","content":"A composition as data, so someone else can retune it without forking you"},"1163":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-1","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/bundles#patches","content":"Patches"},"1164":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-2","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/bundles#inspecting","content":"Inspecting"},"1165":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-3","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/bundles#why-this-matters-for-distribution","content":"Why this matters for distribution"},"1166":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-4","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/bundles#next","content":"Next"},"1167":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-5","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles","content":"A sequence of runtime.plugin() calls is a program. You can read it, but you cannot address a line of it from outside — so a consumer who wants one row changed has to fork the package that published it."},"1168":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-6","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles","content":"A bundle is the same composition expressed as data: an ordered list of rows, each with an id."},"1169":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-7","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles#patches","content":"A patch targets a row by id and applies in order, so a deployment tunes a composition it does not own:"},"1170":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-8","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles#patches","content":"Four operations, and that is the whole vocabulary: retune a row's config, swap its plugin, drop it, or add one at a known position."},"1171":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-9","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles#patches","content":"A set or replace against a row that is not there throws rather than silently doing nothing. A composition you cannot trust to have applied is worse than no composition at all — a typo in an id should not become a security policy that quietly stopped applying. (remove of an absent id is a no-op, since \"make sure this is gone\" is already satisfied.)"},"1172":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-10","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles#patches","content":"A later bundle re-declaring an id overrides that row in place rather than appending a duplicate, so extends composes without reordering surprises."},"1173":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-11","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles#inspecting","content":"Two views, for when it does not behave:"},"1174":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-12","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles#inspecting","content":"The first is static: the resolved row list after patches, before anything runs. The second is live: the fiber tree, including any plugin still blocked on a dependency. Between them, \"why is my plugin not doing anything\" is usually a one-line answer."},"1175":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-13","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles#inspecting","content":"Exposing describeBundle on a --dump-config flag is a good habit — it turns \"what is this harness made of\" into something a user can answer themselves:"},"1176":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-14","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles#inspecting","content":"Every row it prints is addressable by a patch."},"1177":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-15","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles#why-this-matters-for-distribution","content":"If you publish a harness, bundles are what let someone adopt 90% of it. They keep your tools, your prompt sections, and your policy, and point the execution seam at their own infrastructure — without a fork that goes stale the moment you ship a fix."},"1178":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-16","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles#why-this-matters-for-distribution","content":"That is the same shape as accepting a config object, except the surface is every row rather than the keys you thought to expose."},"1179":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-17","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles#next","content":"Case study: mini-harness — a bundle with twelve rows, and what each proves"},"1180":{"id":"/docs/extend/bundles-18","page_id":"/docs/extend/bundles","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/bundles#next","content":"Plugin kernel — the plugin() calls a bundle compiles into"},"1181":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","type":"page","content":"Capability seams","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Extending"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/capabilities"},"1182":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-0","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities","content":"The services a harness is actually made of — shells, jobs, commands, todos, web, LSP and the rest"},"1183":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-1","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"Execution"},"1184":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-2","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#persistent-terminals","content":"Persistent terminals"},"1185":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-3","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#background-work","content":"Background work"},"1186":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-4","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#human-interaction","content":"Human interaction"},"1187":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-5","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#commands","content":"Commands"},"1188":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-6","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#asking-the-user-something","content":"Asking the user something"},"1189":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-7","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#task-tracking","content":"Task tracking"},"1190":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-8","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"Knowledge"},"1191":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-9","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#protecting-the-context-window","content":"Protecting the context window"},"1192":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-10","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#images","content":"Images"},"1193":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-11","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#deterministic-work","content":"Deterministic work"},"1194":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-12","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#next","content":"Next"},"1195":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-13","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities","content":"The kernel and the runtime give you a turn. What makes it a harness is the capability layer on top: the things an agent can actually do, each behind a seam so you can swap the implementation without touching the tools."},"1196":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-14","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities","content":"Every service here is an ordinary plugin. Mount what you need, replace what you don't like."},"1197":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-15","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"service"},"1198":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-16","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"what it owns"},"1199":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-17","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"reference provider"},"1200":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-18","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"ctx.fs"},"1201":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-19","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"every file touch"},"1202":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-20","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"nodeExecution, memoryFileSystem()"},"1203":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-21","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"ctx.subprocess"},"1204":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-22","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"where a process runs"},"1205":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-23","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"nodeExecution, scriptedSubprocess()"},"1206":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-24","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"ctx.sandbox"},"1207":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-25","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"argv confinement, applied last"},"1208":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-26","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"argvSandbox([...])"},"1209":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-27","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"ctx.shell"},"1210":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-28","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"shell dialect and environment"},"1211":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-29","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"localShell, scriptedShell()"},"1212":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-30","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"ctx.terminals"},"1213":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-31","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"persistent interactive sessions"},"1214":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-32","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"nodeTerminals, scriptedTerminals()"},"1215":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-33","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"ctx.shell sitting above ctx.subprocess is deliberate. The shell backend owns dialect — bash versus pwsh, which managed variables exist, how a command line becomes argv. ctx.subprocess owns where the process runs. Keeping them apart is what lets a Windows composition swap the dialect without touching the sandbox, and a remote composition move execution without touching the dialect."},"1216":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-34","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#execution","content":"To confine commands by the operating system instead, mount sandboxedShell from @agentkit/workspace in place of localShell. Every command runs in root whatever cwd it asks for, can write only there (plus writableRoots), has no network unless network: true, and the system prompt tells the model so (prompt: false turns that off). It uses the best confinement available (bubblewrap, sandbox-exec, hardened local); pass executor to choose."},"1217":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-35","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#persistent-terminals","content":"A one-shot shell loses everything between calls: a cd, an activated virtualenv, an SSH session, a REPL. That is the right default — reproducible and cheap — which is exactly why the stateful case needs its own seam rather than a flag on the stateless one."},"1218":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-36","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#persistent-terminals","content":"nodeTerminals is backed by pipes, not a PTY. A real terminal needs node-pty, a native module, and making the runtime need a compiler to get a bash tool is a bad trade. Pipes keep cwd and exported environment — which is the reason to want a persistent session at all. What they do not give you is a TTY: no job control, no line editing, and programs checking isatty() take their non-interactive branch. Mount a node-pty-backed provider when a session needs those; the tools do not change."},"1219":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-37","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#background-work","content":"ctx.jobs is kind-agnostic on purpose. A background shell command, a detached subagent and a long terminal send are the same shape once started — something producing output that a model wants to poll, read, and stop — so three tools cover producers that did not exist when they were written."},"1220":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-38","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#background-work","content":"Reads are incremental by default, so polling a chatty job does not re-send its history. sink.exit() is optional: a producer with no exit-code concept simply never calls it, and the record keeps exitCode undefined rather than claiming a 0 it did not earn. A settled job reaches the model through agent.inject() rather than requiring it to remember to poll."},"1221":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-39","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#commands","content":"ctx.commands is for things a person asks the harness to do directly. /compact is a decision the user already made; spending a model request to re-derive it is waste."},"1222":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-40","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#commands","content":"dispatch returns undefined for anything it does not own, so the fall-through to the model is explicit. A throwing command becomes an error outcome rather than taking down whatever dispatched it."},"1223":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-41","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#asking-the-user-something","content":"ctx.userQuestions is distinct from tools/ask, which answers one fixed question (\"may this tool run?\"). This carries an arbitrary question with its own options, so plan approval, a disambiguation prompt and a credential request are all one mechanism a UI implements once."},"1224":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-42","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#asking-the-user-something","content":"With no provider mounted it declines rather than blocking. An unattended run must not hang forever on a person who is not there."},"1225":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-43","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#task-tracking","content":"allowParallelInProgress is required with no default: it changes the tool description the model reads, and a harness should state which working style it wants rather than inherit one."},"1226":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-44","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#task-tracking","content":"The list is not projected into model history. It reaches the model as a prompt section rendering current state, so a hundred edits cost one rendering, while the log still holds every revision for a UI to replay."},"1227":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-45","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"service"},"1228":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-46","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"tools"},"1229":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-47","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"note"},"1230":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-48","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"ctx.web"},"1231":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-49","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"web_search, web_fetch"},"1232":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-50","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"fetchWeb covers fetch; bring your own search key"},"1233":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-51","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"ctx.lsp"},"1234":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-52","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"lsp"},"1235":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-53","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"definition / references / hover / diagnostics / symbols"},"1236":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-54","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"ctx.sessionQuery"},"1237":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-55","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"session_search, session_trace, session_list"},"1238":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-56","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"read-only, over past logs"},"1239":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-57","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"Grep finds a string; an index finds a symbol. The difference matters most where an agent is weakest — a real call site rather than a comment mentioning it, the type error before the build runs."},"1240":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-58","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#knowledge","content":"Both web and lsp follow one rule: the tools stay registered with no provider mounted and fail with a structured WEB_UNAVAILABLE / LSP_UNAVAILABLE the model can read and route around. A schema that appears and disappears with deployment configuration makes the same session behave differently for reasons the model cannot see — and it defeats prompt caching."},"1241":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-59","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#protecting-the-context-window","content":"A grep matching four thousand lines is not a bug — the answer really is four thousand lines. Truncating throws away what the model needed; inlining costs the window. Spilling keeps the whole result addressable and returns a head plus a locator."},"1242":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-60","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#protecting-the-context-window","content":"It is a tools/post-execute listener rather than a per-tool feature, deliberately: a tool author should not have to think about the context window, and a policy that only covers the tools that remembered to opt in is not a policy. The canonical value is untouched — code and Code Mode still get everything. Only what the model reads is capped."},"1243":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-61","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#images","content":"Bytes stay out of the message stream; the log records that an attachment was added. Execution refuses unless the routed model declares image input:"},"1244":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-62","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#images","content":"Per-model rather than per-provider, because one provider's models rarely agree. An adapter that declares nothing is treated as text-only: guessing wrong sends a request that fails on the wire, which is worse than declining locally."},"1245":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-63","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#deterministic-work","content":"A loop lets the model choose the next action; a workflow fixes it in advance. Both are useful, and the mistake is making one pretend to be the other. Steps declare needs, so independent ones run concurrently without the author scheduling anything."},"1246":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-64","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#deterministic-work","content":"The graph is validated in register(), not at run time — a cycle or a dangling needs is a mistake in the definition, and it should fail while its author is looking at it."},"1247":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-65","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#next","content":"Configuration — settings, hooks, credentials, profiles"},"1248":{"id":"/docs/extend/capabilities-66","page_id":"/docs/extend/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/capabilities#next","content":"Case study: mini-harness — these seams assembled into an agent"},"1249":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","type":"page","content":"Code Mode","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Extending"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/code-mode"},"1250":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-0","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode","content":"The model writes a program instead of a call — and where that program runs is a seam"},"1251":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-1","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/code-mode#where-the-program-runs-is-a-separate-decision","content":"Where the program runs is a separate decision"},"1252":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-2","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/code-mode#what-nodecoderuntime-actually-does","content":"What nodeCodeRuntime actually does"},"1253":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-3","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/code-mode#when-not-to-use-it","content":"When not to use it"},"1254":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-4","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/code-mode#next","content":"Next"},"1255":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-5","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode","content":"A model that can only make one tool call per step spends a step filtering a list, another chaining a result into the next call, and a third summarising. Code Mode collapses that: every visible tool becomes await tools.<name>(args) inside a single run_code program."},"1256":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-6","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode","content":"What the model writes:"},"1257":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-7","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode","content":"Three tool calls and a filter, in one step, one result."},"1258":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-8","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode","content":"Calls resolve to the tool's canonical value — the object execute returned — rather than its rendered prose, which is exactly what makes .filter() above work. That is the tool contract earning its keep. Signatures go into the system prompt, generated from the same schemas the tools already declare, so a new tool is scriptable the moment it is registered."},"1259":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-9","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode","content":"Tool calls made by a program re-enter the ordinary pipeline, so policy, guards, metrics, and the session log apply to them exactly as they would to a direct call. A denied tool throws a catchable error inside the program rather than silently returning nothing."},"1260":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-10","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode#where-the-program-runs-is-a-separate-decision","content":"This is the part worth reading twice."},"1261":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-11","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode#where-the-program-runs-is-a-separate-decision","content":"Running model-authored code in the host process gives it the host's reach no matter what the plugin intends. new Function is one expression away from process and node:fs, which bypasses ctx.fs, workspaceRoot, the permission gate, and the sandbox — and none of that I/O is logged as a tool call. Every guarantee on the other pages of this section evaporates at once."},"1262":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-12","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode#where-the-program-runs-is-a-separate-decision","content":"So codeRuntime is a seam, and codeMode refuses to load without one. Nobody gets code execution by forgetting a plugin; runtime.pending() will tell you it is waiting."},"1263":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-13","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode#what-nodecoderuntime-actually-does","content":"It runs programs in a node:vm context holding only their bindings. The subtle part is that a host function handed into a vm is itself an escape — its .constructor compiles in the host realm, so binding.constructor(\"return process\")() walks straight back out."},"1264":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-14","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode#what-nodecoderuntime-actually-does","content":"The bindings are therefore built inside the vm, around a single host callback captured in a closure, with arguments crossing as JSON. Inside a program, globalThis holds only tools and log; import is blocked, process is undefined, and an infinite loop is cut off by the vm timeout."},"1265":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-15","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode#what-nodecoderuntime-actually-does","content":"node:vm is still not a hard security boundary. It raises the cost of an escape from one expression to a V8 bug. For genuinely untrusted input — a program authored by a model that a third party is prompting — implement CodeRuntime against a separate process or container. That is what the seam is for."},"1266":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-16","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode#when-not-to-use-it","content":"Code Mode adds a scripting surface and a prompt section. If the agent makes one or two independent tool calls per step, it earns nothing. It pays off on fan-out (query many, keep few), chaining (each call's output feeds the next), and iteration over a result set."},"1267":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-17","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode#next","content":"Execution seams — the guarantees Code Mode must not bypass"},"1268":{"id":"/docs/extend/code-mode-18","page_id":"/docs/extend/code-mode","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/code-mode#next","content":"Tool contract — the canonical value programs consume"},"1269":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","type":"page","content":"Configuration","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Extending"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/configuration"},"1270":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-0","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration","content":"Profiles, layered settings, shell-out hooks, and credentials — retuning a harness without forking it"},"1271":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-1","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/configuration#profiles","content":"Profiles"},"1272":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-2","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/configuration#layered-settings","content":"Layered settings"},"1273":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-3","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/configuration#keys-are-flat","content":"Keys are flat"},"1274":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-4","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/configuration#provenance","content":"Provenance"},"1275":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-5","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"Hooks"},"1276":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-6","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/configuration#credentials","content":"Credentials"},"1277":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-7","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/configuration#next","content":"Next"},"1278":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-8","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration","content":"Everything so far is about what a harness can do. This page is about how somebody else deploys and retunes yours without forking it."},"1279":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-9","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#profiles","content":"Bundles answer \"what does this feature mount\". A profile answers \"what does this deployment boot\" — and they are different questions, because the same bundles make a CLI, a server and a one-shot runner depending on what else is stacked and in which order."},"1280":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-10","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#profiles","content":"Layers apply in one order: each bundle in listed order, then the profile's own patches, then whatever the caller passes. A profile is exactly a bundle whose extends is its bundle list, so there is one set of layering rules to reason about rather than two."},"1281":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-11","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#profiles","content":"describeProfile() prints the resolved rows, not the declaration — what the layers produced, not what each layer hoped for. Every row it prints is addressable by a patch."},"1282":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-12","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#layered-settings","content":"Four layers by convention — defaults (0) → home (10) → project (20+) → flags (30). Project files are searched upward from cwd, applied outermost-first, so a nested package's settings beat the repo root's, the same way .gitignore and tsconfig.json already behave. Anyone editing one of these files is relying on that intuition."},"1283":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-13","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#keys-are-flat","content":"model.id, not { model: { id } }. Merging nested objects across layers means deciding, per key, whether a layer replaces or extends what is under it — and every codebase that has tried has ended up with both and no way to tell which you are getting. Nested JSON files are flattened on load; arrays stay whole, because a partial array merge has no obvious meaning."},"1284":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-14","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#provenance","content":"This is the question a layered config always has to answer at 2am, which is why explain() exists alongside get(). describe() lists the mounted layers; override() sets a value in a layer of its own, so a runtime change is visibly an override rather than an edit to somebody else's file."},"1285":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-15","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#provenance","content":"A malformed settings file fails loudly at load. Silently ignoring it would mean a typo'd JSON file makes a policy quietly stop applying — the failure mode worth the least tolerance."},"1286":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-16","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"A hook is not a plugin: it is a line of config pointing at a script. That is the whole point — \"run the formatter after every edit\" or \"never let it touch .env\" should not require writing TypeScript, publishing a package, or restarting anything."},"1287":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-17","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"Events: PreToolUse, PostToolUse, UserPromptSubmit, Stop. The hook receives a JSON payload on stdin."},"1288":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-18","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"the script does"},"1289":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-19","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"the harness does"},"1290":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-20","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"exits non-zero"},"1291":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-21","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"deny; stderr becomes the reason the model reads"},"1292":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-22","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"prints JSON {\"decision\":\"deny\",\"reason\":\"…\"}"},"1293":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-23","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"deny, with that reason"},"1294":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-24","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"prints JSON {\"context\":\"…\"}"},"1295":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-25","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"inject it as model-visible context"},"1296":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-26","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"prints anything else"},"1297":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-27","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"treat it as context"},"1298":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-28","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"exits zero, prints nothing"},"1299":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-29","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"allow"},"1300":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-30","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"Non-JSON stdout becoming context rather than an error matters: echo is what people reach for first, and a stray one must not read as a refusal. A Stop hook that denies is asking for another step — that is how a \"did you run the tests?\" gate keeps the agent working."},"1301":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-31","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#hooks","content":"Hooks are implemented entirely on the existing waterfalls — a hook is one more tools/pre-execute listener. That was the real test of whether those extension points were general enough."},"1302":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-32","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#credentials","content":"A seam rather than scattered process.env reads, for three reasons: a hosted deployment usually has a vault instead of an environment, a missing key should be one clear failure rather than an adapter-specific one, and a value that never becomes a plain property is much harder to log by accident."},"1303":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-33","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#credentials","content":"Providers are tried highest-priority first and fall through on a miss, so a keychain sits in front of the environment without either knowing about the other. require() names what it tried, and unresolved() lists everything something asked for and did not get — the first thing to check when a provider mysteriously will not authenticate."},"1304":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-34","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#credentials","content":"envCredentials maps { scope: \"anthropic\", key: \"apiKey\" } to ANTHROPIC_API_KEY."},"1305":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-35","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#next","content":"Capability seams — what the settings and hooks are configuring"},"1306":{"id":"/docs/extend/configuration-36","page_id":"/docs/extend/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/configuration#next","content":"Bundles — the layer profiles are built from"},"1307":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","type":"page","content":"Context engineering","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Extending"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering"},"1308":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-0","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"Deferred tools, approval by annotation, compaction, spill, dynamic subagents, and what the loop does on its own"},"1309":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-1","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#tool-annotations-and-selectors","content":"Tool annotations and selectors"},"1310":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-2","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#deferred-tool-loading","content":"Deferred tool loading"},"1311":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-3","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#approval-policy","content":"Approval policy"},"1312":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-4","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#compaction","content":"Compaction"},"1313":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-5","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#large-results","content":"Large results"},"1314":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-6","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#dynamic-subagents","content":"Dynamic subagents"},"1315":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-7","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#what-the-loop-does-on-its-own","content":"What the loop does on its own"},"1316":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-8","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#not-ported-and-why","content":"Not ported, and why"},"1317":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-9","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"Everything the model sees on a step is context: the system prompt, the tool\nschemas, the history, the tool results. Too little and the agent guesses; too\nmuch and reasoning degrades while cost climbs. The runtime ships a set of\nplugins that keep that balance, each an ordinary plugin on the public\nextension points, each removable."},"1318":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-10","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"Several of these follow the design of TrueForge\n(MIT), an open-source agent harness whose context-engineering layer we read\nclosely: deferred tool loading, the compaction prompt, large-result\noffloading, annotation-based approvals, and model-authored subagents. Where\nAgentKit's behaviour differs on purpose, this page says so. The grand tour exercises every plugin here in a runnable example."},"1319":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-11","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"plugin"},"1320":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-12","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"what it does"},"1321":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-13","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"extension points it uses"},"1322":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-14","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"deferredTools"},"1323":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-15","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"Tool search: above 30 tools, keeps the catalogue out of every request; the model searches for a tool when it needs it."},"1324":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-16","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"tools.defer, four meta-tools, a prompt section"},"1325":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-17","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"approvalPolicy"},"1326":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-18","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"Asks, allows, or refuses by what a tool declares about itself."},"1327":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-19","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"tools/pre-execute, tools.guard"},"1328":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-20","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"compaction"},"1329":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-21","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"Replaces a long history with a model-written brief, in the log."},"1330":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-22","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"agent/pre-step, agent/request-error, context/compacted"},"1331":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-23","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"spillPolicy"},"1332":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-24","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"Caps what the model reads of a large result; the value stays whole."},"1333":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-25","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"tools/post-execute, ctx.spill"},"1334":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-26","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"dynamicSubagents"},"1335":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-27","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"Lets the model delegate a task it wrote itself, in parallel."},"1336":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-28","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"ctx.subagents, one tool, a prompt section"},"1337":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-29","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"currentDateTime"},"1338":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-30","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"Tells the model the date."},"1339":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-31","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"a prompt section"},"1340":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-32","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"loop: open-call closer"},"1341":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-33","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"Answers tool calls a dead turn left unanswered."},"1342":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-34","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"built into agentLoopPlugin"},"1343":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-35","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"loop: parallelToolCalls"},"1344":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-36","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"Runs one step's calls concurrently."},"1345":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-37","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","content":"AgentOptions"},"1346":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-38","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#tool-annotations-and-selectors","content":"A tool can say what it does to the world:"},"1347":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-39","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#tool-annotations-and-selectors","content":"annotation"},"1348":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-40","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#tool-annotations-and-selectors","content":"meaning"},"1349":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-41","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#tool-annotations-and-selectors","content":"readOnly"},"1350":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-42","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#tool-annotations-and-selectors","content":"Reads only; changes nothing the caller can observe."},"1351":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-43","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#tool-annotations-and-selectors","content":"destructive"},"1352":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-44","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#tool-annotations-and-selectors","content":"May delete or irreversibly change something."},"1353":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-45","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#tool-annotations-and-selectors","content":"idempotent"},"1354":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-46","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#tool-annotations-and-selectors","content":"Calling it twice with the same arguments equals calling it once."},"1355":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-47","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#tool-annotations-and-selectors","content":"delegates"},"1356":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-48","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#tool-annotations-and-selectors","content":"Runs other tools through the pipeline; policy applies to those, not to this wrapper."},"1357":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-49","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#tool-annotations-and-selectors","content":"Policies take lists of selectors: literal names or the tags @all,\n@read-only, @write, @destructive."},"1358":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-50","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#tool-annotations-and-selectors","content":"One deliberate difference from TrueForge: there, an unannotated tool matches\nno tag but @all. Here an unannotated tool matches @write. Most tools in a\ncodebase say nothing about themselves, and a policy of \"ask before writes\"\nthat silently skipped all of them would not be a policy. Declare readOnly\nto opt a tool out."},"1359":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-51","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#deferred-tool-loading","content":"Mounted with no options, deferredTools does nothing for a small tool set. Once the\nregistry holds more than 30 tools or about 10k tokens of definitions, every tool\nexcept alwaysLoad is deferred: the model's request carries only the loaded schemas plus the\nmeta-tools, and the system prompt lists what exists. The meta-tools are sent only while\nsomething is deferred."},"1360":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-52","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#deferred-tool-loading","content":"A deferred tool stays registered and callable, so ctx.tools.execute and\nCode Mode still reach it. What changes is ctx.tools.schemas()."},"1361":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-53","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#deferred-tool-loading","content":"meta-tool"},"1362":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-54","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#deferred-tool-loading","content":"purpose"},"1363":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-55","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#deferred-tool-loading","content":"search_tools"},"1364":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-56","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#deferred-tool-loading","content":"Keyword search by what the model needs to do (\"merge a pull request\", \"slack\"): the best matches with their input schemas, ready for call_tool."},"1365":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-57","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#deferred-tool-loading","content":"list_tools"},"1366":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-58","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#deferred-tool-loading","content":"Names and short descriptions of the deferred tools, optionally by category."},"1367":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-59","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#deferred-tool-loading","content":"get_tool_info"},"1368":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-60","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#deferred-tool-loading","content":"One tool's description, input schema, category and annotations."},"1369":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-61","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#deferred-tool-loading","content":"call_tool"},"1370":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-62","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#deferred-tool-loading","content":"Run a deferred tool by name. The inner call goes through the full pipeline, so guards, tools/pre-execute, hooks, metrics and the session log see it as they would a direct call."},"1371":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-63","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#deferred-tool-loading","content":"A prompt section lists the catalogue: every tool with a short description, or, past 30\ndeferred tools, one line per MCP server or category with a count and example names.\nctx.tools.defer(predicate) is the registry primitive underneath; a realm inherits its\nparent's deferrals and cannot undo them. createAgent has the same behaviour built in as\ntoolSearch."},"1372":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-64","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#approval-policy","content":"allow wins over requireApproval. deny is registered as a guard, so no\nlater tools/pre-execute listener, however permissive, can lift it. A\ntool with delegates: true (call_tool, create_subagent) is passed\nthrough: the calls it makes are gated one by one. Questions go out on\ntools/ask; with no responder mounted, the call is declined."},"1373":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-65","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#compaction","content":"Before each step the plugin reads the last request's input tokens (the\nprovider's own count, from step/end). Past the trigger it asks the model\nfor a structured brief (intent, decisions, files and snippets, errors and\nfixes, pending tasks, current work, next step) and appends it to the log as\ncontext/compacted. The projection then collapses everything before that\nboundary into the brief plus a continuation instruction. Nothing is deleted;\na resumed session sees what the model saw."},"1374":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-66","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#compaction","content":"The same path handles a provider's \"context window exceeded\" error: compact,\nthen let the loop retry. compaction/done reports what happened."},"1375":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-67","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#compaction","content":"The trigger derivation (a ratio of the context window, or a fixed count) and\nthe shape of the summary prompt follow TrueForge's compaction capability."},"1376":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-68","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#large-results","content":"spillPolicy already capped any single result. It now also:"},"1377":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-69","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#large-results","content":"shows the head and the tail of a spilled result, so the model sees the\nshape of the end as well as the start;"},"1378":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-70","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#large-results","content":"truncates an error message instead of spilling it, because an error is\nfor the model to read now, not fetch later;"},"1379":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-71","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#large-results","content":"applies a per-step budget (maxStepChars): several results that each fit\ncan still flood a request together, and once a step's inline total passes\nthe budget, later results in that step are spilled too."},"1380":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-72","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#large-results","content":"TrueForge offloads the largest results first once a step's total is over\nbudget. AgentKit's tool calls run sequentially by default and each result is\ncapped as it arrives, so the budget spills the later results instead; with\nparallelToolCalls the effect is the same."},"1381":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-73","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#dynamic-subagents","content":"create_subagent takes a name, a self-contained task, and (when a model set\nis configured) which model to use. The child gets a fixed identity, the\nparent's tools, none of the parent's conversation, and returns only its final\nanswer. Delegation is one level deep unless allowNesting is set. Several\ncreate_subagent calls in one step run concurrently when the parent was\ncreated with parallelToolCalls: true."},"1382":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-74","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#dynamic-subagents","content":"Predefined subagents (ctx.subagents.define plus subagentTool) remain the\nright choice when the delegate's identity and tool set are a design\ndecision rather than something the model should improvise."},"1383":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-75","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#what-the-loop-does-on-its-own","content":"Open tool calls. A turn that dies after logging a tool/call but before\nits tool/result leaves the model's last message with unanswered calls,\nwhich every provider rejects on the next request. At the start of each turn\nthe loop appends a failed tool/result for each such call, telling the\nmodel to retry if still needed. It is logged rather than synthesised at\nprojection time: model-visible means logged."},"1384":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-76","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#what-the-loop-does-on-its-own","content":"Parallel tool calls. agents.create({ parallelToolCalls: true }) runs\none step's calls concurrently. Every tool/call is logged first, then all\nresults in call order once every call has settled, so the log stays\ndeterministic. The default stays sequential."},"1385":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-77","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#not-ported-and-why","content":"Durable approvals. TrueForge ends the turn when a tool needs approval\nand resumes on a later request that carries the decision. AgentKit's\ntools/ask waits in-flight. A durable version needs a logged\ntool/approval-required fact and a resume path in the loop; it is the\nright next step and is scoped separately."},"1386":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-78","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#not-ported-and-why","content":"Sandbox-as-a-tool with an in-sandbox MCP bridge. AgentKit keeps the\nsandbox behind the fs and subprocess seams and runs Code Mode against\na CodeRuntime. Bridging tool calls from inside a remote sandbox back to\nthe harness over a transport is a different design, not a port."},"1387":{"id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering-79","page_id":"/docs/extend/context-engineering","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/context-engineering#not-ported-and-why","content":"Token-usage breakdown (harness vs instructions vs tools vs messages)\nis a good diagnostic; it needs per-section token estimates from the prompt\nservice and is left for a follow-up."},"1388":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","type":"page","content":"Execution seams","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Extending"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/execution"},"1389":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution-0","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/execution","content":"Filesystem, subprocess, and sandboxing — swap the whole execution world in one move"},"1390":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution-1","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/execution#confinement-is-not-a-provider","content":"Confinement is not a provider"},"1391":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution-2","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/execution#process-confinement","content":"Process confinement"},"1392":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution-3","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/execution#testing-without-a-disk","content":"Testing without a disk"},"1393":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution-4","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/execution#next","content":"Next"},"1394":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution-5","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/execution","content":"ctx.fs and ctx.subprocess are where every file and process touch goes. Nothing else in the tree talks to a disk or spawns anything, which is what makes the execution world swappable in one move."},"1395":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution-6","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/execution","content":"A tool calls ctx.fs.readFile(path); it does not know whether that lands on a local disk, a container, or an object store. That indirection is the point — a tool written for a laptop runs unmodified against a remote sandbox."},"1396":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution-7","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/execution#confinement-is-not-a-provider","content":"Path limits and read-only modes are deliberately not part of a provider. They are fs/access listeners:"},"1397":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution-8","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/execution#confinement-is-not-a-provider","content":"Keeping policy out of the provider means swapping the provider does not silently lose the policy — the two are composed, not fused. And because the check lives at the seam, no tool can forget it."},"1398":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution-9","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/execution#process-confinement","content":"Process confinement is a SandboxProvider that rewrites argv after every subprocess/spawn listener has had its say:"},"1399":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution-10","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/execution#process-confinement","content":"The ordering is the security property. A subprocess/spawn listener can vet or rewrite a command, but the sandbox rewrite happens last, so no plugin can rewrite its way out of it — and swapping the spawner provider does not drop the confinement."},"1400":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution-11","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/execution#testing-without-a-disk","content":"These are the reference providers, and they make an entire harness testable with no disk and no processes. mini-harness does exactly this: its tests swap the execution world for an in-memory one and every file and shell tool follows, with no tool change — which is the proof that the seam is real rather than nominal."},"1401":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution-12","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/execution#next","content":"Code Mode — why where a program runs is a seam of its own"},"1402":{"id":"/docs/extend/execution-13","page_id":"/docs/extend/execution","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/execution#next","content":"Tool contract — the tools that sit on top of these"},"1403":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","type":"page","content":"Case study: the grand tour","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Extending"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour"},"1404":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-0","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","content":"Every feature of the SDK in one bundle, walked in twenty-two verified stops"},"1405":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-1","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour#the-stops","content":"The stops"},"1406":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-2","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour#how-it-is-built","content":"How it is built"},"1407":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-3","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour#what-a-stop-looks-like","content":"What a stop looks like"},"1408":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-4","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour#in-live-mode","content":"In live mode"},"1409":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-5","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","content":"examples/grand-tour composes the whole plugin spine in one bundle and walks it in twenty-two stops. Each stop is one prompt, a scripted model response, and a set of checks against the session log, the journal and the requests the runtime actually built. It runs with no API key on a scripted model, and the same stops are its test suite."},"1410":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-6","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","content":"Where mini-harness proves the extension points are enough for a coding agent, the tour proves every battery works when mounted together, and shows what each one looks like from the outside: the tool calls, the injected context, the events a UI would render."},"1411":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-7","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour#the-stops","content":"#"},"1412":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-8","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour#the-stops","content":"stop"},"1413":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-9","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour#the-stops","content":"feature"},"1414":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-10","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour#the-stops","content":"rows in the bundle"},"1415":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-11","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour#the-stops","content":"1"},"1416":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-12","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour#the-stops","content":"One bundle, every feature"},"1417":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-13","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour#the-stops","content":"composition as data"},"1418":{"id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour-14","page_id":"/docs/extend/grand-tour","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/grand-tour#the-stops","content":"runtime, model"},"