end","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#packages","content":"@agentkit/kernel"},"1605":{"id":"/docs/extend-94","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#packages","content":"contexts, services, reversible effects, inject, a five-mode event bus, bundles. Nothing agent-specific."},"1606":{"id":"/docs/extend-95","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#packages","content":"@agentkit/runtime"},"1607":{"id":"/docs/extend-96","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#packages","content":"the agent microkernel on that kernel: session log, tool pipeline, prompt assembly, model seam, replaceable loop."},"1608":{"id":"/docs/extend-97","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#packages","content":"@agentkit/core"},"1609":{"id":"/docs/extend-98","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#packages","content":"both of the above, plus the Node-side providers and every battery — nodeExecution, nodeTerminals, fetchWeb, fileSpill, settingsFiles, sessionPersistence, nodeCodeRuntime, hooks."},"1610":{"id":"/docs/extend-99","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#packages","content":"Edge and worker targets can depend on @agentkit/runtime alone; the Node-only pieces (SQLite persistence, node:vm Code Mode, real fs/subprocess) live in @agentkit/core."},"1611":{"id":"/docs/extend-100","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#next-steps","content":"Plugin kernel — contexts, effects, inject, dispatch modes, realms"},"1612":{"id":"/docs/extend-101","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#next-steps","content":"Agent runtime — services, the turn, every extension point"},"1613":{"id":"/docs/extend-102","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#next-steps","content":"Session log — why model-visible means logged"},"1614":{"id":"/docs/extend-103","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#next-steps","content":"Tool contract — one definition, four consumers"},"1615":{"id":"/docs/extend-104","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#next-steps","content":"Capability seams — shells, jobs, commands, todos, web, LSP, spill"},"1616":{"id":"/docs/extend-105","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#next-steps","content":"Execution seams — filesystem, subprocess, sandboxing"},"1617":{"id":"/docs/extend-106","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#next-steps","content":"Code Mode — the model writes a program instead of a call"},"1618":{"id":"/docs/extend-107","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#next-steps","content":"Context engineering — deferred tools, approval by annotation, compaction, spill, dynamic subagents"},"1619":{"id":"/docs/extend-108","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#next-steps","content":"Bundles — a composition as data, patchable by id"},"1620":{"id":"/docs/extend-109","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#next-steps","content":"Configuration — profiles, layered settings, hooks, credentials"},"1621":{"id":"/docs/extend-110","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#next-steps","content":"Case study: mini-harness — a coding agent built only from these APIs"},"1622":{"id":"/docs/extend-111","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#next-steps","content":"Case study: the grand tour — every feature in one bundle, twenty-two verified stops"},"1623":{"id":"/docs/extend-112","page_id":"/docs/extend","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend#next-steps","content":"Building a coding agent — what the spine gives a Claude Code-style product, what stays yours, what is missing"},"1624":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","type":"page","content":"Plugin kernel","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Extending"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/kernel"},"1625":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-0","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel","content":"Contexts, reversible effects, inject, dispatch modes, and realms"},"1626":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-1","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/kernel#a-context-with-services","content":"A context with services"},"1627":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-2","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/kernel#reversible-effects","content":"Reversible effects"},"1628":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-3","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/kernel#inject--dependencies-across-time","content":"inject — dependencies across time"},"1629":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-4","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"Five dispatch modes"},"1630":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-5","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/kernel#realms","content":"Realms"},"1631":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-6","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/kernel#services-and-thisctx","content":"Services and this.ctx"},"1632":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-7","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/kernel#config","content":"Config"},"1633":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-8","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/kernel#next","content":"Next"},"1634":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-9","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel","content":"@agentkit/kernel is the composition substrate under AgentKit. Nothing in it is agent-specific — it is contexts, services, plugin lifecycles, and events. You do not need it to use AgentKit; you need it to extend AgentKit without asking anyone's permission."},"1635":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-10","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel","content":"Everything on this page is also re-exported from @agentkit/core."},"1636":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-11","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel","content":"runtime.start() resolves once the tree has settled — every plugin whose dependencies exist has loaded."},"1637":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-12","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#a-context-with-services","content":"A service published at ctx.<name> is reachable by every plugin in the tree. Augment the Services interface to type it:"},"1638":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-13","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#a-context-with-services","content":"After that, ctx.tools is typed everywhere, with no registry lookup and no casts."},"1639":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-14","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#reversible-effects","content":"Everything a plugin registers is owned by that plugin and unwinds when it unloads — in reverse order, so a later effect that depends on an earlier one is torn down first."},"1640":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-15","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#reversible-effects","content":"ctx.on, ctx.provide, and every registry method built on ctx.effect inherit this, so a plugin author almost never writes cleanup by hand. Unloading a plugin genuinely removes it — no orphaned listeners, no stale services."},"1641":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-16","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#inject--dependencies-across-time","content":"A dependency here is a relationship over time, not a startup-order constraint. The plugin loads when its dependencies appear, unloads when one disappears, and reloads when it comes back."},"1642":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-17","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#inject--dependencies-across-time","content":"An optional dependency does not block loading but still triggers a reload when it changes, so \"use telemetry if it is mounted\" is a first-class relationship rather than a nullable lookup scattered through the body."},"1643":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-18","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#inject--dependencies-across-time","content":"runtime.pending() lists what has not loaded and what it is waiting for — the first thing to check when a plugin seems inert."},"1644":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-19","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"mode"},"1645":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-20","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"shape"},"1646":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-21","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"emit"},"1647":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-22","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"fire and forget; a throwing listener is contained"},"1648":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-23","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"parallel"},"1649":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-24","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"await all, aggregate failures"},"1650":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-25","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"serial"},"1651":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-26","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"sequential, first non-nullish result wins"},"1652":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-27","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"bail"},"1653":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-28","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"synchronous short-circuit"},"1654":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-29","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"waterfall"},"1655":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-30","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"middleware chain; each listener receives next()"},"1656":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-31","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"waterfall is the one that turns an observation channel into an extension point. A waterfall carries one named input object, so adding a field later never breaks the plugins already listening on it:"},"1657":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-32","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#five-dispatch-modes","content":"Listener order is explicit rather than accidental — pass { prepend: true } to run before the plugins already registered."},"1658":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-33","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#realms","content":"ctx.isolate(name) gives a subtree its own instance of one service. Everything else still comes from the tree above it."},"1659":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-34","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#realms","content":"This is how a restricted subagent genuinely cannot reach its parent's tools, rather than being asked nicely not to. A derived tool registry inherits its parent's guards (so invariants cannot be escaped by moving into a realm) while owning its own filters (so narrowing stays local)."},"1660":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-35","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#services-and-thisctx","content":"A service is read through the reader's own context, so a registration made inside a service method belongs to the calling plugin:"},"1661":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-36","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#services-and-thisctx","content":"Because callers see a per-context view, keep mutable service state in fields holding objects (Map, Set, array, or a shared object). this.items.set(k, v) mutates the shared instance; this.count = 1 would write to one caller's view and be invisible to everyone else."},"1662":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-37","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#config","content":"A plugin may declare a schema. Zod works directly, and so does anything else with a parse:"},"1663":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-38","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#config","content":"A config that fails to parse fails that plugin's fiber and leaves the rest of the tree running — one bad row does not take down the harness."},"1664":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-39","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#next","content":"Agent runtime — the services and extension points built on this"},"1665":{"id":"/docs/extend/kernel-40","page_id":"/docs/extend/kernel","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/kernel#next","content":"Bundles — the same composition expressed as patchable data"},"1666":{"id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness","page_id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness","type":"page","content":"Case study: mini-harness","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Extending"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/mini-harness"},"1667":{"id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness-0","page_id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/mini-harness","content":"A terminal coding agent built only from public plugin APIs"},"1668":{"id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness-1","page_id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/mini-harness#what-each-plugin-proves","content":"What each plugin proves"},"1669":{"id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness-2","page_id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/mini-harness#composition","content":"Composition"},"1670":{"id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness-3","page_id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/mini-harness#tests-as-the-real-proof","content":"Tests as the real proof"},"1671":{"id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness-4","page_id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/mini-harness#the-live-smoke-test","content":"The live smoke test"},"1672":{"id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness-5","page_id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/extend/mini-harness#next","content":"Next"},"1673":{"id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness-6","page_id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/mini-harness","content":"examples/mini-harness is a terminal coding agent assembled only from public AgentKit plugin APIs. It is also the acceptance test for this whole section: every capability below is an ordinary plugin, and none of them patches the loop, the tool registry, or the session log."},"1674":{"id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness-7","page_id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/mini-harness","content":"If a feature here had needed a change inside @agentkit/runtime, the extension points would not be finished."},"1675":{"id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness-8","page_id":"/docs/extend/mini-harness","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/extend/mini-h