run through the same approval and reasoning loop as developer tools."],[377,"AgentKit has a configured level and a compositional one. createAgent(config) is the first: configuration in, agent out. Under it is a plugin kernel where every part of a turn is a service you can replace or an event you can intercept — see Extending AgentKit. The concepts below describe the configured level; both run the same tool pipeline and the same session log."],[427,"AgentKit headless agent runtime"],[414,"AgentKit is a headless TypeScript agent runtime. createAgent(config) validates config with Zod at construction. The engine ships providers (Anthropic, OpenAI, Ollama, Gemini, Mistral, Groq, mock), tools, memory, MCP, workflows, and adaptive encoding."],[431,"AgentKit is a TypeScript agent runtime you import into existing projects. The engine runs on Node; any UI connects over the AG-UI wire."],[1076,"AgentKit is built for that shape at the plugin level, not the config level. createAgent and @agentkit/workspace still work for the simple case, but a product like this is a harness, and the spine is where a harness lives: createRuntime, agentRuntime(), and plugins on public extension points. Two examples prove it and are the right starting points:"],[418,"AgentKit ships a single-file guide written for AI coding assistants: every feature, the full config, events, the server/client/hook APIs and recipes. Install it into your project and your assistant builds with the SDK instead of hand-rolling servers, reconnects or approval queues:"],[605,"AgentKit Studio (studio)"],[799,"AgentKit surfaces runtime activity as typed AG-UI events rather than only stdout. Set logging.level to control log emissions from the agent shell, and subscribe to cost:update for per-turn token and cost estimates from the active provider."],[572,"AgentKit uses config over code at every level:"],[245,"AgentMode"],[1345,"AgentOptions"],[1815,"agentRuntime() deliberately mounts only the spine. These ship alongside it and